<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mammad Amin Rasulzade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mammad Amin Rasulzadeh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mammad Amin Rasulzade was an Azerbaijani politician and writer, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musavat">Musavat</a> party and chairman of the national council that proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> Democratic Republic in 1918, the first parliamentary republic in the Muslim world. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> overthrew the republic in 1920 he went into exile and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mammad-amin-rasulzade.jpg" alt="Mammad Amin Rasulzade" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dilgam Ahmad Collection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahammad_Amin_Rasulzade_5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mahammad Amin Rasulzade was born on 31 January 1884 in Novkhany, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, and studied at the Russian-Muslim Secondary School and the Technical College in Baku. While a student he created the Muslim Youth Organisation Musavat, the first secret organisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>'s modern history. From 1903 he wrote for opposition newspapers, and in 1904 he founded Hummet, the first Muslim social-democratic organisation, editing its papers Takamul and Yoldash. He took an active part in the First Russian Revolution of 1905 to 1907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Persecuted by the tsarist authorities, he fled in 1909 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, where he edited the newspaper Iran-e Azad, helped found the Democrat Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and published Iran-e Now. After Russian troops entered Iran in 1911 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and founded the journal Türk Yurdu. Under the Amnesty Act of 1913 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musavat">Musavat</a> party, which had been established in 1911 and which he soon led. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> it became a leading party of the Caucasian Muslims.</p><p>On 28 May 1918 the Azerbaijani National Council, headed by Rasulzade, declared an independent Azerbaijan Republic. His phrase, The flag once raised shall never fall, became the motto of the independence movement. He also initiated the founding of Baku State University in 1919, with funds from the oil magnate Haji Zeynalabdin Taghiyev, and taught Ottoman literature there. After the republic collapsed in April 1920 he went into hiding in the village of Lahıc to direct resistance to Sovietization. He was arrested in August 1920, but was released, said to be because he had once hidden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> from the police, and was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rasulzade escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> in 1922 and lived the rest of his life in exile, first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, where he edited the magazine Yeni Kafkasya from 1923 to 1927. He was expelled from Turkey in 1931 and later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. In 1942 the leadership of Nazi Germany approached him, and in May 1942 he sought an alliance to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>'s independence, demanding a commitment to the restoration of the Transcaucasian states, which the Reich evaded. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> in 1947. He died in 1955 and was buried in the Cebeci Asri cemetery in Ankara.</p><p>Baku State University was named in his honour, and he appeared on the Azerbaijani 1000 manat banknote of 1993 to 2006. A high school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> also bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahammad Amin Rasulzade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic was led by the Musavat party under <b>Mammad Amin Rasulzade</b> and prime ministers such as Fatali Khan Khoyski, with a multi-party parliament that included minority representatives.</blockquote><a href="/state/azerbaijan-democratic-republic">Azerbaijan Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Novkhany, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.54,49.78&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Novkhany on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mammad Amin Rasulzade"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4340 4074h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4340 4074h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.54,49.78&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
